# Copy this file to `.env` in the same directory, edit, then:
#
#   docker compose up -d
#
# Compose reads `.env` from this directory automatically. Everything below is
# optional except QUORUM_IMAGE, which has a working default.

# --- Image -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The published image. `latest` follows every push to main.
QUORUM_IMAGE=ghcr.io/brendentaylor22/quorum:latest

# Prefer an immutable digest for anything you rely on: it is the only way to
# know that what you restart tonight is what you scanned this morning. Every
# GitHub Release records the digest it was built from.
#
#   QUORUM_IMAGE=ghcr.io/brendentaylor22/quorum@sha256:REPLACE_WITH_APPROVED_DIGEST

# Named volume holding the SQLite database. Renaming this points Quorum at a
# different (probably empty) database; it does not move the old one.
#QUORUM_DATA_VOLUME=quorum-data

# --- Ingress -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The app publishes no port and has no route to the Internet, so something in
# front of it has to terminate TLS and forward. Pick one shape.
#
#   An ingress you already run — the common case on a host that serves
#   something else. Nothing to set here. Plain `docker compose up -d`, then
#   attach your proxy to the `quorum-edge` network and forward it to
#   http://quorum:3000. Never attach Quorum to the proxy's network.
#
#   The bundled Caddy reverse proxy, which obtains its own certificates.
#   Requires both variables below, and ports 80/443 reaching this host.
#   Collides with an existing proxy, because it publishes 80 and 443.
#
#     docker compose --profile proxy up -d
#
#   Cloudflare Tunnel, opening no inbound port at all. Put the credential at
#   ./secrets/tunnel-credentials.json and the hostname in
#   ./cloudflared/config.yml.
#
#     docker compose --profile tunnel up -d
#
# See docs/self-hosting.md for all three in full.

# Required by the `proxy` profile only. Must resolve to this host.
#QUORUM_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME=quorum.example.org
#QUORUM_ACME_EMAIL=you@example.org

# Whose `X-Forwarded-For` to believe. Unset means nobody's, which is correct
# only with nothing in front of Quorum.
#
# Set this behind ANY of the three shapes above, or every request looks like it
# came from the proxy, one rate-limit bucket serves the whole household, and the
# first busy room locks everyone else out. Do not set it with nothing in front:
# a caller would then set the header themselves and every limit becomes
# decorative.
#
# Accepts `true`, a hop count, or — best — the addresses/CIDRs you actually
# trust. The Docker bridge range suits a proxy in a neighbouring container;
# `true` is the usual answer for the Cloudflare tunnel.
#QUORUM_TRUST_PROXY=172.16.0.0/12

# --- Who may start a room ------------------------------------------------------
# `public` (the default) lets anyone who loads the page create a room. That is
# right for a private address and wrong for a hostname strangers and crawlers
# can reach, where it is an open invitation to fill the database with rooms
# nobody asked for.
#
# `operator` closes `POST /api/rooms` — the landing page then says the instance
# is invitation-only — and leaves the CLI as the only way to start one:
#
#   scripts/quorumctl session          # starts the stack, prints the host link
#   docker compose exec app node apps/api/dist/cli.js create-room
#
# The first person to open the host link becomes the host; a second is refused.
# Everyone else joins with the invite phrase. See docs/self-hosting.md.
#QUORUM_ROOM_CREATION=operator

# Public origin, used only to print whole links instead of paths. Quorum never
# builds a link from this at request time — invite links come from the address
# the host actually used — so a stale value costs a wrong line in the terminal,
# nothing more.
#QUORUM_PUBLIC_URL=https://quorum.example.org

# --- Tuning ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Multiplies every rate limit. 1 is the documented policy; raise it only if a
# full room of phones on one address trips limits. 0 disables limiting and is
# defensible only on a trusted private network.
#QUORUM_RATE_LIMIT_SCALE=1

# How often expiry and retention deletion run, in minutes.
#QUORUM_RETENTION_SWEEP_MINUTES=15

# --- Catalog import ----------------------------------------------------------
# Read only by the `catalog-refresh` container, and only when you run it. Quorum
# ships a 60-movie fixture catalog, so none of this is needed to play.
#
#   docker compose --profile refresh run --rm catalog-refresh
#
# Needs a TMDB v4 read access token at ./secrets/tmdb-read-access-token.secret.
#QUORUM_CATALOG_MIN_VOTES=600
#QUORUM_CATALOG_FIRST_YEAR=1930
#QUORUM_CATALOG_MAX_ITEMS=30000
#QUORUM_CATALOG_RATING_PRIOR=3000
#QUORUM_CATALOG_CONCURRENCY=12
#QUORUM_CATALOG_REGIONS=GB,US
